== The Hogsmeade Candy Shop Saga

Honeydukes is a candy shop in Hogsmeade. The shop makes two types of popular candy, Chocolate Frogs ($F$) and Ice Mice ($M$). Devoting all their resources to one good, Honeydukes can make $100$ pounds of $F$ or $75$ pounds of $M$ each week.

== Q1 | Honeydukes' PPF

a) Set up Honeydukes' weekly PPF on an $x,y$ graph with Ice Mice ($M$) on the vertical and Chocolate Frogs ($F$) on the horizontal.

b) What is Honeydukes' opportunity cost of $1$F?

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
    *b)* $\frac{3}{4}$ pound of M.

      *a)* A full week on Ice Mice is the vertical intercept, $75$ M; a full week on Chocolate Frogs is the horizontal intercept, $100$ F. The PPF is the line between them, $M = 75 - \frac{3}{4} F$. The three Q2 orders are marked.

      *b)* A week is $100$F or $75$M, so $100F = 75M$ and

      #mitex(`
      1F = \tfrac{75}{100} M = \tfrac{3}{4} M
      `)

      Flip it for the other good: $1M = \frac{4}{3} F$. The $\frac{3}{4}$ is also the absolute slope of the PPF: Ice Mice given up per extra pound of Frogs.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q2 | Feasibility

The shop receives three owl-orders for next week. Classify each production plan as inefficient, efficient, or unattainable, using your graph or algebra to justify your answers.

a) $40$F and $30$M

b) $60$F and $30$M

c) $80$F and $30$M

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *a)* Inefficient. *b)* Efficient. *c)* Unattainable.

  *Graph:* all three orders sit on the $30$M line. The frontier allows $M = 75 - \frac{3}{4} F$, which is $45$ at $F = 40$, $30$ at $F = 60$, and $15$ at $F = 80$. So $(40, 30)$ is below the frontier, $(60, 30)$ is on it, and $(80, 30)$ is above it.

  *Algebra (time check):* $30$M takes $\frac{30}{75} = \frac{2}{5}$ of the week in every order. The Frogs take $\frac{40}{100} = \frac{2}{5}$, $\frac{60}{100} = \frac{3}{5}$, and $\frac{80}{100} = \frac{4}{5}$ of the week, for totals of $\frac{4}{5}$, $1$, and $\frac{6}{5}$ weeks. Under a full week is inefficient, exactly one week efficient, over one week unattainable. Either justification earns full credit.
]

